meta {
  name: Backtest
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/swift-scan/backtest/tqqq?date=2026-10-06
  body: none
  auth: none
}

params:query {
  date: 2026-10-06
  ~timeframe: M5
  ~sessions: RTH
  ~count: 2000
  ~debug: true
}

headers {
  Accept: application/json
}

docs {
  # Swift Trend Scanner — Backtest

  Replays a historical day for ONE ticker and reports exactly WHEN the Swift
  Trend Scanner would have fired a swift UP or DOWN signal. Uses the SAME config
  as the live scanner (webull.alerts.swift-scan + webull.supertrend), so the
  output matches what you'd see live. Detection/analysis only — no orders, no
  notifications.

  Pure Supertrend flip + momentum (no EMA): a swift signal = a fresh Supertrend
  flip within `flip-within-bars`, backed by >= `min-travel-atr` ATR of travel
  since the flip, a widening separation >= `min-separation-atr` ATR, enough
  strong directional candles, and NOT in a whipsaw (`max-flips-in-window`).

  ## Path
  - `/{ticker}` — any US stock symbol (need NOT be in the universe).

  ## Query
  - `date`      — ET calendar date to analyse, YYYY-MM-DD (default: today ET).
  - `timeframe` — Webull timeframe (default: configured swift-scan.timeframe).
                  Supported: M1, M5, M15, M30, M60, M120, M240, D, W, M, Y.
  - `sessions`  — RTH, PRE, ATH, OVN (comma-separated; default: configured).
  - `count`     — bars to fetch back from NOW. Auto-sized by default to reach
                  the date with Supertrend warm-up. The broker returns the most
                  recent N bars (no date range), so OLDER dates need a larger
                  `count`. The response's `coverage` shows the range reached and
                  whether the date was in range.
  - `debug`     — true to include `debugTrace`: a per-bar row for the whole date
                  with the detector's metrics + reason, so you can see WHY each bar
                  did or didn't fire (and tune thresholds). Same detection code;
                  nothing filtered.

  ## Response (200) — simple audit
  {
    ticker, date, timeframe,
    currentSupertrend: { supertrend, direction (UP|DOWN), close, atr, barTimeEt },
    signalCount, swiftUpCount, swiftDownCount,
    signals: [ "09:35 ET  🔴 DOWN  @ 229.80  ST 231.40 (DOWN)",
               "13:05 ET  🟢 UP  @ 236.10  ST 234.00 (UP)", ... ],
    warmup: { warmupBarsUsed, barsOnTargetDate, totalBarsFetched,
              supertrendLength, warmupSufficient }
  }

  `currentSupertrend` is the latest COMPLETED Supertrend on the configured timeframe,
  computed from the fetched series (which runs up to now) — i.e. the live value and
  direction right now, with the bar's ET time.

  `warmup` reports the seeding used going into the day:
  - `warmupBarsUsed`    — candles BEFORE the target date, used to seed Supertrend/ATR.
  - `barsOnTargetDate`  — candles ON the date that were actually replayed.
  - `supertrendLength`  — min bars Supertrend needs to produce a value.
  - `warmupSufficient`  — true when warmupBarsUsed >= supertrendLength. If false, a
                          `warmupNote` is included and early-day signals may be
                          unreliable — increase `count`.

  `signals` is a flat, chronological list of the signals the scanner would have
  caught — each line is the ET time of the COMPLETED bar it fired on (the same
  moment the live scanner would alert), the direction + emoji, and the price. The
  per-direction cooldown is applied during the replay, so you see "fire once per
  flip" rather than every qualifying bar. An empty list means no swift move that
  day.

  ## Debug trace (when `debug=true`)
  `debugTrace: [ { timeEt, close, direction, flipBarsAgo, travelAtr,
                   separationAtr, strongCandles, flipsInWindow, swift,
                   wouldFire, reason } ]`
  One row per bar on the date. `reason` says exactly which check rejected a bar
  (e.g. "travel 1.6667×ATR < 2.5", "separation ... < 1.5", "whipsaw: ...",
  "latest flip not in current direction"). Compare the rows over the move you
  expect to catch to see what to loosen.

  ## Notes
  - A `note` field appears when the date is out of the fetched range (increase
    `count`) or when there were no bars on the date (weekend/holiday/no trades).

  ## Errors
  - `400` — invalid date/timeframe/session.
  - `502` — Webull market-data error.
  - `503` — no bars returned for the ticker.
}
